as a k-12 educator I often get questions  from parents about how they can foster  their child's composition instruction at  home here's a couple of suggestions to  keep in mind that you can do  first off read have your students read  as much as possible the more that they  read the more they're exposed to  vocabulary and sentence structures that  they might not otherwise have been  exposed to so you want to encourage that  facet of reading as much as possible it  will pay large dividends and their  ability to write later on secondly  formalize your speech if you don't want  your students to write using sentence  fragments or using split infinitives  then be intentional about the way that  you talk to them students mimic the  source of communication that they hear  and read around them if you want that to  play out in the students own writing  make sure that that's a sort of  communication that they hear from you as  well thirdly get them talking about  things ask them about the sorts of  things which they're reading about or  the sorts of things that they're  learning about their other subjects this  is the opportunity for your students to  tell you about the things that they've  learned probe more deeply with questions  ask them why and ask them how they know  those particular things the more that  your students have the experience of  articulating their thoughts the more  that would communicate to their written  composition as well  fourth give them opportunities to write  does give you something as simple as a  letter to a family member or a grocery  list but give your student the  opportunity to actually write and to  write with you the more they have this  opportunity the less daunting it will  seem when they're sitting down to the  written composition of their own that  they need to compose and finally give  them a place in the home that they're  able to write have some portion of the  home be well-stocked with pencils and  papers and take this opportunity to  write with your students as well if you  have a daily practice where you journal  this could be a great opportunity to set  your child next to you and to have them  see you as you are writing each and  every day the more that you put these  sorts of practices into place the less  daunting of a task composition can be  and the more your student can thrive as  a writer  you
 

